course: dictionary
lesson: 13039
title: A fanfare of words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A fanfare is a short, loud, exciting tune played on trumpets or other brass instruments. It is played to announce someone important or the start of something big. «The trumpets gave a fanfare as the queen walked in.» The loud tune tells everyone that someone important has arrived.
explain: The word can also mean a lot of excitement and showy celebration. «The new phone was launched with great fanfare.» Nobody had to play trumpets for this to be true: the launch was loud, bright and full of publicity. Here, fanfare means a big show of excitement.
explain: Look for clue words around the word. «The team won the cup, and the town greeted them with a fanfare of cheers and balloons.» Cheers and balloons are not trumpets, so fanfare must mean a big, showy welcome. Check by swapping in "a big noisy celebration": if the sentence still makes sense, you have found the meaning.
explain: Sometimes the clue is a word like without. «The secret door opened without fanfare.» Without fanfare means quietly, with no show at all. The word without tells you the opposite of a big celebration.
fact: A fanfare is a short, loud tune played on trumpets or other brass instruments.
fact: Fanfares are often played to announce a king, a queen or an important ceremony.
fact: «Fanfare» can also mean lots of excitement and publicity, as in «with great fanfare».
fact: «Without fanfare» means quietly, with no show or celebration.
fact: When you talk about the excitement, you say fanfare alone, not «a fanfare».
exercise: «The trumpets gave a fanfare as the king entered the hall.» What is a fanfare here? | A type of heavy door | A quiet song sung by the king | A long walk through the hall | *A short, loud tune played on trumpets
exercise: «The new film was released with great fanfare.» What does fanfare mean here? | A loud argument | *Lots of excitement and showy publicity | Very quiet and secret | A tiny, boring change
exercise: «The little boy slipped into the room without fanfare.» What does without fanfare mean? | With a big party and balloons | *Quietly, with no show | Angrily and fast | With a loud tune and trumpets
test: «The village welcomed the explorers with a fanfare of cheers and bells.» What does fanfare suggest? | *A noisy, joyful celebration | A sleepy afternoon nap | A dark, quiet night
test: Which phrase means the same as «without fanfare»? | Quickly and angrily | *Quietly, with no fuss | Loudly, with trumpets
exam: Which sentence uses fanfare to mean a short, loud tune? | *«The bugler played a fanfare to start the race.» | «She shouted her fanfare across the field.» | «The fanfare was a sleepy cat in the garden.»
